The expression ${x : P(x)}$ cannot be formed for arbitrary properties $P$. Suppose $X = {x : x "is a set" and x in.not x}$ were a set: if $X in X$ then by definition $X in.not X$, while if $X in.not X$ then $X in X$ by construction. There is therefore no universal set $cal(U)$ with $x in cal(U)$ for all $x$; otherwise $X$ could be formed as ${x in cal(U): x in.not x}$. A set is guaranteed to exist when it is obtained from known sets such as $NN$ or $RR$ by the constructions above.
